READELF(1)                  User Commands                READELF(1)

NAME
    readelf - display the contents of ELF files

SYNOPSIS
    readelf [-aeghlSsrdnuVAIW] [--dyn-syms] [--got-contents]
            [-x section] [-p section] file...

DESCRIPTION
    readelf prints the headers, tables and sections of ELF64 files of
    x86_64 and aarch64 in text form. The input is an executable, a
    shared object, a relocatable object or a System V archive of such
    objects. For an archive, readelf prints the line "File: archive(member)"
    before the output of each member. For several files, readelf prints
    the line "File: file" before the output of each file.

    The output equals the output of GNU readelf -W (version 2.47). readelf
    always uses the wide format. The option -W is accepted and has no
    further effect. Without any selecting option, readelf prints a usage
    message and exits with status 1.

OPTIONS
    -a, --all             select -h -l -S -s -r -u -d -n -V -A -I -g
                          and --got-contents
    -h, --file-header     print the ELF file header
    -l, --program-headers print the program headers and the mapping of
    --segments            sections to segments
    -S, --section-headers print the section headers
    --sections
    -e, --headers         select -h -l -S
    -g, --section-groups  print the section groups
    -s, --syms            print the symbol tables (.symtab and .dynsym)
    --symbols
    --dyn-syms            print the dynamic symbol table only
    -r, --relocs          print the relocation sections
    -d, --dynamic         print the dynamic section
    -n, --notes           print the note sections
    -u, --unwind          report the unwind information. x86_64 and
                          aarch64 have no section that readelf decodes
    -V, --version-info    print the version sections
    -A, --arch-specific   print the AArch64 build attributes
    -I, --histogram       print the bucket lengths of the hash sections
    --got-contents        print the global offset table with the
                          relocation of each entry
    -x, --hex-dump=SECTION
                          print the section as bytes in hexadecimal
    -p, --string-dump=SECTION
                          print the strings of the section
    -W, --wide            accepted for compatibility
    -H, --help            print a usage message
    -v, --version         print the version of the program

    SECTION is a section number or a section name. The options -x and -p
    are repeatable. A section without data gives the message
    "Section 'NAME' has no data to dump."

LIMITS
    readelf does not implement the options -t, -c, -D, -w, -R, -z, -j,
    -C, -U, -X, -T, -L, -P, --lto-syms, --sym-base, --ctf and --sframe.
    readelf decodes the properties of GNU notes for the x86 and AArch64
    features only. readelf does not decode relocation sections of the
    type SHT_RELR, 32-bit files or compressed sections. readelf does not
    decode the unwind sections of x86_64.

EXAMPLES
    readelf -h -l -S /bin/sh
    readelf -s -W libjson.so
    readelf -x .rodata -p .comment prog
    readelf -a libedit.a

SEE ALSO
    objdump(1), nm(1), size(1), strings(1), ar(1)
